Amazon Q
   |
   v
ServiceNow
   |
   v
Support Ticket

A user could potentially request:

Create a support ticket for this issue.

Amazon Q can use the configured integration to send the request to the external system.

Custom integrations can also be created using APIs, allowing organizations to connect internal applications and business workflows.
Step 8: Create Amazon Q Apps

Amazon Q Apps allows users to create AI-powered applications using natural language.

For example, a customer support employee might want an application that:

    Receives a customer complaint.

    Analyzes the complaint.

    Identifies the key issue.

    Generates a professional response.

The user can describe the desired workflow and then customize the resulting application.

Q Apps can also work with organizational information and configured integrations.
Step 9: Use Amazon Q Developer

Amazon Q Developer provides assistance for software development and AWS workflows.

Developers can ask questions such as:

How do I list my Lambda functions?

or:

Why is my Lambda deployment failing?

Amazon Q Developer can assist with:

    Code generation

    Code explanation

    AWS CLI commands

    Troubleshooting

    AWS service questions

    Security-related suggestions

    Development workflows

AI-generated code should still be reviewed, tested, and validated before being used in production.
Step 10: Test Before Production

Testing is one of the most important steps before deploying an enterprise AI assistant.

Organizations should test:

    Authentication

    User permissions

    Data retrieval

    Response quality

    Citations

    Blocked topics

    Plugins

    Error handling

    Different user roles

For example, administrators should test both:

Questions that should return information

and:

Questions involving restricted information

The goal is to verify both functionality and security.
